'''thirl'''<sup>1</sup> (thėrl), ''n.'' [Also ''thurl''; < ME. ''thirl'', ''thirll'', ''therl'', ''thyrl'', *''thorl'', ''thurl'', < AS. ''thyrel'', a hole, perforation, < ''thyrel'', adj., perforated, pierced, orig. *''thyrhel'' = OHG. ''durihhil'', ''durchil'', MHG. ''durchel'', ''durkel'', perforated, pierced; with formative ''-el'', from the root of AS. ''thurh'', etc., thorough, through: see ''thorough'', ''through''. Hence ''thirl''<sup>1</sup>, ''v.'', and by transposition ''thrill''<sup>1</sup>, ''n.'' and ''v.'', and in comp. ''nosethirl'', ''nostril''.] 1. A hole; an opening; a place of entrance, as a door or a window. [Prov. Eng. or Scotch.]

{{smaller block|Thise byeth the vif gates of the cite of the herte, huerby the dieuel geth in ofte ine the vif ''therles'' of the house.
{{right|''Ayenbite of Inwyt'' (E. E. T. S.), p. 204.}}}}

{{smaller block|If thou ware in a myrke house one the daye, and alle the ''thirlles'', dores, and wyndows ware stokyne that na sone myght enter. {{right|''MS. Lincoln'' A. i. 17, f. 241. (''Halliwell.'')}}}}

2. In ''coal-mining'', a short passage cut for ventilation between two headings; a cross-hole. Also ''thirling''.—'''Stoop and thirl.''' {{smaller|See ''stoop''<sup>4</sup>.}}

'''thirl'''<sup>1</sup> (thėrl), ''v.'' [< ME. ''thirlen'', ''thirllen'', ''thyrlen'', ''therlen'', ''thurlen'', ''thorlen'', < AS. ''thyrlian'', ''thirlian'', ''thyrelian'', bore, < ''thyrel'', a hole, perforation: see ''thirl''<sup>1</sup>, ''n.'' Cf. ''thrill''<sup>1</sup>, a transposed form.] I. ''trans.'' 1. To pierce; bore; perforate; drill.

{{smaller block|Thenn ''thurled'' thay ayther thik side thurȝ, bi the rybbe.
{{right|''Sir Gawayne and the Green Knight'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 1357.}}}}

{{smaller block|That he was myghtful and meke, and mercy gan graunte
To hem that henge hym hye and hus herte ''therlede''.
{{right|''Piers Plowman'' (C), ii. 171.}}}}

2. To produce, as a hole, by piercing, boring, or drilling.

{{smaller block|As also that the forcible and violent push of the ram had ''thirled'' an hole through a corner-tower.}}

