{{smaller block|{{right|''Ammianus Marcellinus'' (1609). (''Nares.'')}}}}

¶
3. Figuratively, to penetrate; pierce, as with some keen emotion; especially, to wound.

{{smaller block|So harde hacches [aches] of loue here hert hadde ''thirled''<br>That ther nas gle vnder God that hire glad miȝt.<br>{{right|''William of Palerne'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 826.}}}}

{{smaller block|The fond desire that we in glorie set<br>Doth ''thirle'' our hearts to hope in slipper hap.<br>{{right|''Mir. for Mags.'', p. 495. (''Nares.'')}}}}

4. To cause to vibrate, quiver, or tingle; thrill.

{{smaller block|There was ae sang, amang the rest; . . .<br>It ''thirl'd'' the heart-strings thro' the breast.<br>{{right|''Burns'', First Epistle to J. Lapraik.}}}}

'''II.''' ''intrans.'' 1. To make a hole, as by piercing or boring.

{{smaller block|So ''thirleth'' with the poynt of remembraunce<br>The swerd of sorowe.<br>{{right|''Chaucer'', Anelida and Arcite, l. 211.}}}}

{{smaller block|Schalkes they schotte thrughe schrenkande maylez,<br>Thurghe brenys browdene brestez they ''thirllede.''<br>{{right|''Morte Arthure'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 1858.}}}}

2. To vibrate; quiver; tingle; thrill.

{{smaller block|Nor that night-wandering, pale, and watery star<br>(When yawning dragons draw her ''thirling'' car . . .).<br>{{right|''Marlowe and Chapman'', Hero and Leander, i. 108.}}}}

{{smaller block|And then he speaks with sic a taking art,<br>His words they ''thirle'' like musick thro' my heart.<br>{{right|''Ramsay'', Gentle Shepherd, i. 2 (song 5).}}}}
